 ],
 "notes": "The list has 34 rows (lines 986–1019), one station per line, in a single column with no wrapped lines. There are no map numbers, marks or light characters. Three rows read _Nil._ (Spurn L.V., Hasbro' L.H., Tongue L.V.), so 31 stations sent returns. That agrees with the other reader's count.\n\nHow I recorded things:\n- Fragments: I left out each line's two-space indent but kept the spacing between the station and keeper columns as printed.\n- Full stops: each row ends with a full stop. I kept it in the fragments but left it off the keepers' names, as in your example \"J. B. Smith\".\n- Keepers joined by \"and\": I split these into separate people, as in your example \"Annie Rose Smith and J. B. Smith\". Galloper has three: \"P. Page and F. Page and C. Rafe\".\n- Coast: the list itself has no sub-headings. I used the section heading (EAST COAST OF ENGLAND.), written as \"East Coast of England\".\n- Hanois: \"Guernsey\" is printed straight after \"L.H.,\" with no column gap before the keeper. I took it as the place, not part of the keeper column.\n\nCopied as printed, but worth checking:\n- \"Leman and Ower L.H.\": this is a light-vessel. The paragraph just above the list (line 979) calls it \"Leman and Ower L.V.\", so \"L.H.\" looks like a misprint.\n- \"Wm. Little wood (wings).\": the surname is printed as two words, probably Littlewood. I put \"(wings)\" in other; it means this vessel sent bird wings (see line 981).\n- \"Tees, 5 Buoy L.V.\": the \"5\" is part of the name (the Tees No. 5 buoy light-vessel), not a map number.\n- Unusual spellings: \"Llyn Wells\" for Lynn Well, \"Languard Point\" for Landguard, \"Hasbro'\" for Happisburgh (spelled in full at line 980), and \"Farn, Inner,\" for Inner Farne.\n- \"East Side L.V.\" is probably the East Goodwin light-vessel; I left it as printed.\n- \"Whitby High\": I treated \"High\" as part of the name.\n- Hanois L.H., Guernsey (Channel Islands) and the Kent and Dover Strait vessels (Goodwin, Gull, South Sand Head, East Side, Varne) are listed under the East Coast heading as the report prints them.",
